.TH SONDE 1 "2026-08-01" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
sonde \- probe the contracted sensors of this ecosystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sonde
.br
.B sonde list
.br
.B sonde contract
[\fIsensor\fR]
.br
.B sonde run
[\fIsensor\fR...]
.br
.B sonde sweep
.br
.B sonde selftest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sonde
probes the sensors declared in the
.B ecosim.*
namespace and prints one reading per line, status symbol first, so that
.BR grep (1)
and
.BR awk (1)
need no parser.
.PP
Its one non\-negotiable behaviour: a reading that could not be taken is
reported as BLIND, never as clean. A sensor that cannot read part of its
domain has not established that the rest of it is fine, and folding those
two states into one symbol is the defect this whole namespace exists to
name.
.PP
.B sonde
is a front door onto tooling that already existed, with one part that is
not a wrapper: the exit translation described under EXIT STATUS. Run
.B sonde
with no arguments for the subcommand list.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B list
The contracted sensors, with each one's symbol count and how many of those
symbols are BLIND symbols.
.TP
.BI contract " sensor"
The declared alphabet of a sensor, what it describes, what files it reads,
and which hosts it claims to cover. With no argument, every sensor.
.TP
.BI run " sensor..."
Probe now. One line per reading. With no argument, every sensor.
.TP
.B sweep
One aggregated pass of every instrument, for an unattended run. The
aggregate is worst\-wins: BLIND beats CRIT beats WARN.
.TP
.B selftest
The coverage gate. Exercises every declared symbol and fails if any symbol
has stopped firing \- a symbol never observed to fire has not been shown
to be capable of firing.
.TP
.B decide
Not offered, and loudly so. The legacy tree carries a
.I decide.sh
menu built for one past research pass; it is a situational script, not a
durable promise, so this subcommand exits 4 and names
.I GAPS.md
rather than running it. It is documented here because a subcommand that
exists and is not on the page fails the page test exactly as hard as one
that is on the page and does not exist.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output, same data. Honoured by
.B run
only, which forwards it to the sensor runtime.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Usage summary.
.SH COST
.B sonde
cannot spend money. It carries no
.B \-\-summon
flag, and no subcommand of it reaches a metered service. Every subcommand
is free and offline.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The tooling behind
.B sonde
speaks the Monitoring Plugins dialect, in which 3 means BLIND. In this
ecosystem 3 means needs\-summon. Passing those codes through unchanged
would report this project's own central finding as a request for money, so
.B sonde
translates every one of them:
.TP
.B 0
The probe was taken and nothing above threshold was observed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. An unknown subcommand is this.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the tooling this subcommand fronts is absent or not executable.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: the underlying tool exited a code its own contract does not define.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: part of the domain could not be read. Translated from the legacy 3.
.TP
.B 8
WARN: a hazard symbol was observed. Project\-specific, above 6 on purpose,
so that "it found something" stays distinct from "it fell over".
.TP
.B 9
CRIT: a critical symbol was observed. Project\-specific, as above.
.PP
Code 3 is deliberately unreachable here. Code 7 is not used by
.B sonde
because
.I CONTRACT.md
already spends it on REFUSED.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the sensors and their alphabets:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sonde list
rotation       12 symbols (4 blind)  hosts=mandark,dexter
quota           6 symbols (2 blind)  hosts=mandark,dexter
boundary        5 symbols (2 blind)  hosts=mandark
sync            6 symbols (2 blind)  hosts=mandark,dexter
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask whether anything could not be read \- the question this tool exists
for. This example and the next read live state, so they assert an exit
code rather than a count, which would go stale between runs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sonde run 2>/dev/null | grep \-q '^BLIND'; echo $?
0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Provoke the BLIND translation, and see it land on 6 rather than 3:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sonde sweep >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?
6
.fi
.RE
.PP
An unknown subcommand is the caller's error, not a gap:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sonde nosuchthing >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I CONTRACT.md
The promise this utility must keep, obligation by obligation.
.TP
.I GAPS.md
What the contract names and the tooling cannot yet do.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SONDE_LEGACY_ROOT
The checkout holding the tooling this verb fronts. Defaults to the
.B ecosim
working tree. This branch does not carry that tooling \- see HISTORY.
.SH HISTORY
Rebuilt 2026\-07\-30 from the tree named
.BR ecosim .
Rewritten 2026\-08\-01: the previous page promised "probe what this system
cannot see, and measure it", which is two clauses and therefore two verbs.
Auditing the mechanisms themselves moved to
.BR ausculte (1).
The old page is in this branch's git history, and the change is named in
the commit that made it.
.PP
This branch is a total purge: it keeps the tool and nothing else. What was
removed is on the default branch of this same repository. That is why this
is a branch and not a new repository, and extracting it into one would
destroy the archive that justifies the purge.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ausculte (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR awk (1)
